the core of the assignment game. In: Leifman, L.J. (ed.): Functional Analysis, Optimization and Mathematical Economics, pp. 274\u2013289, Oxford University Press.","DOI":"10.1016\/B978-0-12-370182-4.50025-8"}],"container-title":["Handbook of the Shapley Value"],"language":"en","deposited":{"date-parts":[[2020,10,15]],"date-time":"2020-10-15T02:55:07Z","timestamp":1602730507000},"score":38.310738,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/www.taylorfrancis.com\/books\/9781351241403\/chapters\/10.1201\/9781351241410-23"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2019,12,6]]},"ISBN":["9781351241410"],"references-count":1,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1201\/9781351241410-23","published":{"date-parts":[[2019,12,6]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,1]],"date-time":"2026-07-01T16:32:15Z","timestamp":1782923535759,"version":"3.54.5"},"edition-number":"1","reference-count":0,"publisher":"Cambridge University Press","isbn-type":[{"value":"9780521406826","type":"print"},{"value":"9780521404938","type":"print"},{"value":"9780511607677","type":"electronic"}],"license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2012,6,5]],"date-time":"2012-06-05T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1338854400000},"content-version":"unspecified","delay-in-days":7299,"URL":"https:\/\/www.cambridge.org\/core\/terms"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"published-print":{"date-parts":[[1992,6,11]]},"DOI":"10.1017\/cbo9780511607677.005","type":"book-chapter","created":{"date-parts":[[2012,6,15]],"date-time":"2012-06-15T18:20:05Z","timestamp":1339784405000},"page":"107-141","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":8,"title":["Fair dealing, just rewards: the ethics of barter in North-East Nepal"],"prefix":"10.1017","author":[{"given":"Caroline","family":"Humphrey","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"56","container-title":["Barter, Exchange and Value"],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.cambridge.org\/core\/services\/aop-cambridge-core\/content\/view\/AB9315973B77CBD61DDA9C012AD030D9","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2022,10,13]],"date-time":"2022-10-13T19:46:17Z","timestamp":1665690377000},"score":36.975285,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/www.cambridge.org\/core\/product\/identifier\/CBO9780511607677A012\/type\/book_part"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[1992,6,11]]},"ISBN":["9780521406826","9780521404938","9780511607677"],"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/cbo9780511607677.005","published":{"date-parts":[[1992,6,11]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,20]],"date-time":"2026-04-20T17:35:59Z","timestamp":1776706559561,"version":"3.51.2"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"group-title":"SSRN","reference-count":0,"publisher":"Elsevier BV","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>This paper examines residential real estate wholesaling (contract assignment) through the lens of two-sided marketplace theory. While commonly characterized as transactional arbitrage, wholesaling can be more accurately understood as a market-making mechanism that connects fragmented supply and demand in local property markets. Drawing on experience building and scaling marketplace platforms in both technology and real estate, this paper analyzes how wholesaling functions as a brokered marketplace that improves liquidity, reduces search costs, and mitigates information asymmetry. The paper explores core marketplace dynamics including network e ects, demand aggregation, and transaction e iciency, and argues that contract assignment models represent an earlystage form of structured marketplace infrastructure within residential real estate.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6605098","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,20]],"date-time":"2026-04-20T16:36:06Z","timestamp":1776702966000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Two-Sided Marketplace Dynamics in Residential Real Estate: An Analysis of Contract Assignment Models and Market Liquidity"],"prefix":"10.2139","author":[{"given":"Stephen","family":"Keighery","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"78","deposited":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,20]],"date-time":"2026-04-20T16:36:06Z","timestamp":1776702966000},"score":36.80905,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/www.ssrn.com\/abstract=6605098"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6605098","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"subtype":"preprint"},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,14]],"date-time":"2026-08-14T10:31:58Z","timestamp":1786703518503,"version":"build-2736575974"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2022]]},"group-title":"SSRN","reference-count":0,"publisher":"Elsevier BV","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>In this paper we introduces a new coalitional game with transferable utility \u2014 called two-sided platform game. The participation of platform user on one side benefits the other side, and the platform can be established if and only if there is more than one entrepreneur. Well-known point solutions and set solutions are investigated. It turns out that the kernel and the nucleolus coincide, and both the Shapley value and the nucleolus have simple expressions. Our paper sheds some lights on the platform antitrust. When there is more than one platform entrepreneur, the utility share of each entrepreneur is relatively low in both point solutions.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.4039043","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2022,3,17]],"date-time":"2022-03-17T14:05:04Z","timestamp":1647525904000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["The Shapley Value and the Nucleolus of a Two-sided Platform Game"],"prefix":"10.2139","author":[{"given":"Jinglei","family":"Huang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Danxia","family":"Xie","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"78","deposited":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,14]],"date-time":"2026-08-14T09:51:04Z","timestamp":1786701064000},"score":35.81068,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/www.ssrn.com\/abstract=4039043"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2022]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.4039043","published":{"date-parts":[[2022]]},"subtype":"preprint"},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2025,6,18]],"date-time":"2025-06-18T04:36:13Z","timestamp":1750221373310,"version":"3.41.0"},"publisher-location":"New York, NY, USA","reference-count":0,"publisher":"ACM","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2018,2,2]],"date-time":"2018-02-02T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1517529600000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/www.acm.org\/publications\/policies\/copyright_policy#Background"}],"content-domain":{"domain":["dl.acm.org"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2018,2,2]]},"DOI":"10.1145\/3159652.3160593","type":"proceedings-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2018,2,6]],"date-time":"2018-02-06T18:12:23Z","timestamp":1517940743000},"page":"801-802","update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/crossmark-policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Workshop on Two-sided Marketplace Optimization"],"prefix":"10.1145","author":[{"given":"Mihajlo","family":"Grbovic","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Airbnb, Inc., San Francisco, CA, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Thanasis","family":"Noulas","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Airbnb, Inc., San Francisco, CA, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"320","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2018,2,2]]},"event":{"name":"WSDM 2018: The Eleventh ACM International Conference on Web Search and Data Mining","sponsor":["SIGMOD ACM Special Interest Group on Management of Data","SIGWEB ACM Special Interest Group on Hypertext, Hypermedia, and Web","SIGKDD ACM Special Interest Group on Knowledge Discovery in Data","SIGIR ACM Special Interest Group on Information Retrieval"],"location":"Marina Del Rey CA USA","acronym":"WSDM 2018"},"container-title":["Proceedings of the Eleventh ACM International Conference on Web Search and Data Mining"],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/10.1145\/3159652.3160593","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3159652.3160593","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2025,6,18]],"date-time":"2025-06-18T02:26:37Z","timestamp":1750213597000},"score":34.401726,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/10.1145\/3159652.3160593"}},"subtitle":["Search,Pricing, Matching &amp; Growth"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2018,2,2]]},"references-count":0,"alternative-id":["10.1145\/3159652.3160593","10.1145\/3159652"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3159652.3160593","published":{"date-parts":[[2018,2,2]]},"assertion":[{"value":"2018-02-02","order":2,"name":"published","label":"Published","group":{"name":"publication_history","label":"Publication History"}}]},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,11]],"date-time":"2026-08-11T17:40:43Z","timestamp":1786470043058,"version":"build-2736575974"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2010]]},"group-title":"SSRN","reference-count":25,"publisher":"Elsevier BV","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>In 2000s regulation in the USA and subsequently in Europe responded to the needs of investors and traders, allowing them to take advantage of modern technology, and unleashing competition never before witnessed in the stock exchange industry, with a singular focus on liquidity. By 2010 new exchanges in the USA and Europe proved successful in the battle for liquidity. Essential to their success was the adoption of a simple and efficient business model of two-sided platforms, which contrasts with and threatens the model of diversification and vertical integration embraced by incumbent exchanges. The increased competition and network effects in the industry have been accompanied by its continued concentration in the New York City area in the USA, and emerging dominance of London in Europe, driven primarily by the value of proximity between exchanges and their major customers in terms of computers and human relations. While their iconic incumbent exchanges may suffer, revolution in the stock exchange industry benefits New York City and London as financial centres.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.1653827","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2012,1,4]],"date-time":"2012-01-04T20:55:22Z","timestamp":1325710522000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Revolution in the Stock Exchange Industry: Two-Sided Platforms, Battle for Liquidity, and Financial Centres"],"prefix":"10.2139","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2158-284X","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Dariusz","family":"Wojcik","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"78","reference":[{"key":"ref1","article-title":"Bank for International Settlements (2010) Triennial Central Bank Survey of Foreign Exchange and Derivatives Market Activity in 2007 - Final Results","author":"J Authers","year":"2007","journal-title":"Financial Times Special Report on World Financial Centres and Exchanges"},{"key":"ref2","article-title":"Exchanges seek a new model","author":"H Biglari","year":"2007","journal-title":"Financial Times Special Report on World Financial Centres and Exchanges"},{"key":"ref3","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1017\/cbo9780511770470.003","author":"J.-P Casey","year":"2009","journal-title":"The MiFID Revolution"},{"key":"ref4","author":"A Capaldo","year":"2008","journal-title":"What's next for exchanges. The McKinsey Quaterly"},{"key":"ref5","first-page":"1780","author":"Y Cassis","year":"2006","journal-title":"Capitals of Capital: A History of Intarnational Financial Centres"},{"key":"ref6","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.1117232","author":"R J Davies","year":"2008","journal-title":"MiFID and a changing competitive landscape"},{"key":"ref7","author":"T R Eisenmann","year":"2009","journal-title":"Opening Platforms: How, When and Why? Harvard Business School Working Paper"},{"key":"ref8","article-title":"Strategies for two-sided markets","author":"T R Eisenmann","year":"2006","journal-title":"Harvard Business Review"},{"key":"ref9","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"679","DOI":"10.1093\/jeg\/lbp027","article-title":"Stock exchange virtualisation and the decline of second-tier financial centres - the cases of Amsterdam and Frankfurt","volume":"9","author":"E Engelen","year":"2009","journal-title":"Journal of Economic Geography"},{"issue":"1","key":"ref10","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"151","DOI":"10.3386\/w11603","article-title":"The industrial organization of markets with two-sided platforms","volume":"3","author":"D S Evans","year":"2007","journal-title":"Competition Policy International"},{"key":"ref11","author":"G Ferrarini","year":"1998","journal-title":"European Securities Markets: The Investment Services Directive and Beyond"},{"key":"ref12","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.1570646","author":"J Hengelbrock","year":"2009","journal-title":"Fourteen at one blow: The market entry of Turquoise"},{"issue":"10","key":"ref13","article-title":"The future of Europe's financial centres","author":"K Lannoo","year":"2007","journal-title":"European Capital Markets Institute Policy Brief"},{"key":"ref14","author":"R Lee","year":"1998","journal-title":"What is an Exchange? The Automation, Management, and Regulation of Financial Markets"},{"key":"ref15","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"297","DOI":"10.1006\/jfin.2002.0343","article-title":"The economics of stock exchange listing fees and listing requirements","volume":"11","author":"J R Macey","year":"2002","journal-title":"Journal of Financial Intermediation"},{"key":"ref16","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","DOI":"10.1093\/acprof:oso\/9780199280612.001.0001","author":"R C Mitchie","year":"2006","journal-title":"The Global Securities Market: A History"},{"key":"ref17","author":"R C Mitchie","year":"1999","journal-title":"The London Stock Exchange: A History"},{"key":"ref18","article-title":"The new masters of Wall Street","author":"L Moyer","year":"2009","journal-title":"Forbes.com"},{"key":"ref19","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"229","DOI":"10.1016\/j.finmar.2008.07.002","article-title":"Stock exchange merger and liquidity: The case of Euronext","volume":"12","author":"U Nielsson","year":"2009","journal-title":"Journal of Financial Markets"},{"key":"ref20","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"177","DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.141048","article-title":"The changing microstructure of European equity markets","author":"M Pagano","year":"1998","journal-title":"European Securities Markets: The Investment Services Directive and Beyond"},{"key":"ref21","author":"Sami Consulting","year":"2009","journal-title":"An Assessment of the City's ICT Infrastructure"},{"key":"ref22","author":"D Verdier","year":"2002","journal-title":"Moving Money: Banking and Finance in the Industrialized World"},{"issue":"2","key":"ref23","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"200","DOI":"10.1111\/j.1468-2257.2007.000364.x","article-title":"Geography and the future of stock exchanges: Between real and virtual space","volume":"38","author":"D W\ufffdjcik","year":"2007","journal-title":"Growth and Change"},{"key":"ref24","author":"P Young","year":"1999","journal-title":"Capital Market Revolution: The Future of Markets in an Online World"},{"key":"ref25","author":"Z\/Yen","year":"2010","journal-title":"Global Financial Centres 7"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,11]],"date-time":"2026-08-11T16:57:59Z","timestamp":1786467479000},"score":34.31385,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/www.ssrn.com\/abstract=1653827"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2010]]},"references-count":25,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.1653827","published":{"date-parts":[[2010]]},"subtype":"preprint"},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,12]],"date-time":"2026-08-12T12:32:08Z","timestamp":1786537928364,"version":"build-2736575974"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2012]]},"group-title":"SSRN","reference-count":34,"publisher":"Elsevier BV","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>&lt;div&gt;\n (Not the final draft. For the final version, see:&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Two-Sided Matching via Balanced Exchange, Journal of Political Economy, 2019)&lt;\/span&gt;\n&lt;\/div&gt;We introduce a new matching model to mimic two-sided exchange programs such as tuition and worker exchange, in which each firm has to avoid being a net-exporter of workers. These exchanges use decentralized markets, making it difficult to achieve a balance between exports and imports. We show that stable equilibria discourage net-exporting firms from exchange. We introduce the two-sided top-trading-cycles mechanism that is balanced-efficient, worker-strategy-proof, acceptable, and individually rational, and respects priority bylaws regarding worker eligibility. We prove that it is the unique mechanism fulfilling these objectives. Moreover, it encourages exchange, since full participation is the dominant strategy for firms.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.2180357","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2012,11,28]],"date-time":"2012-11-28T11:01:18Z","timestamp":1354100478000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":2,"title":["Two-Sided Matching via Balanced Exchange: Tuition and Worker Exchanges"],"prefix":"10.2139","author":[{"given":"Umut","family":"Dur","